  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has solutions. We have $a=3,$ $b=7,$ and $c=11.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(3,7)=1\n$$\n\nand $1\\,|\\,11,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation II has no solution. We have $a=-3,$ $b=-9,$ and $c=2.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-3,-9)=3\n$$\n\nand $3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,2,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation III has solutions. We have $a=-12,$ $b=15,$ and $c=21.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-12,15)=3\n$$\n\nand $3\\,|\\,21,$ our equation has solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
